# Line endings, per file type — and for one file type this is load-bearing.
#
# cmd.exe refuses to run a .bat file whose lines end in LF alone. Measured on
# Windows 10.0.26300: an LF-only batch file exits 255 with no output at all. This
# is not a style preference, it is the difference between working and not running.
#
# The global Git configuration on this host is core.autocrlf=input, which only
# rewrites files on the way INTO the index and never on checkout. Without the rule
# below, a clone of this repository produces an LF-only clean-bom-senior.bat and
# the batch port is broken for everyone. Verified before adding this file:
#   git ls-files --eol clean-bom-senior.bat  ->  i/lf  w/crlf  attr/
# and again after it:                              i/lf  w/crlf  attr/text eol=crlf

# Batch files: always CRLF in the working tree, on every platform.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Shell scripts: always LF, so a host with core.autocrlf=true cannot break them.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.bash text eol=lf

# PowerShell and the project's own text files are edited and compared as LF. This
# is what the tools themselves produce, and what the differential tests hash.
*.ps1 text eol=lf
*.psm1 text eol=lf
*.js text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf

# Binary assets must never be normalised.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ico binary
*.zip binary
